{"metadata": {"team_id": "cfda", "run_id": "gen-only_npsg13_thru0_d4c5", "run_type": "generation-only"}, "turn_id": "1-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers are both used to treat acid-related conditions but work in different ways. PPIs, such as omeprazole, inhibit the proton pump in the stomach, significantly reducing acid production, making them more effective for chronic conditions like gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). They provide longer-lasting relief but take longer to work, typically requiring daily use before meals. In contrast, H2 blockers, like ranitidine, block histamine receptors that stimulate acid production, offering quicker relief (within 15-30 minutes) but with a shorter duration of action (up to 12 hours). While PPIs are generally preferred for severe cases due to their superior effectiveness in healing erosive esophagitis and managing symptoms, H2 blockers may be suitable for milder symptoms and are often less expensive. Overall, PPIs are favored for more severe or long-term treatment, while H2 blockers may be used for less severe, short-term relief.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:9": 6.848442077636719, "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 6.412575721740723, "clueweb22-en0035-42-00345:14": 6.249649524688721, "clueweb22-en0006-26-13955:3": 6.202922344207764, "clueweb22-en0024-51-00969:0": 6.009904861450195, "clueweb22-en0024-49-15168:1": 5.868865489959717, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 5.8514933586120605, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 5.725640296936035, "clueweb22-en0041-39-13388:3": 5.619043350219727, "clueweb22-en0045-30-11232:7": 5.597897529602051, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 5.581158638000488, "clueweb22-en0039-93-00517:10": 5.576419830322266, "clueweb22-en0001-77-05768:12": 5.528866767883301}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:9": 6.848442077636719, "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 6.412575721740723, "clueweb22-en0035-42-00345:14": 6.2496495246887