{"metadata": {"team_id": "cfda", "run_id": "gen-only_npsg13_thru0_d4c5", "run_type": "generation-only"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The pain in your finger after climbing could be related to injuries in the tendons, pulleys, or joints, which are common in climbers. When you grip or crimp during climbing, significant stress is placed on the tendons and the pulleys that support them. This can lead to pulley tears, which cause localized pain in the finger, or tendon injuries that can extend pain to the wrist.\n\nTendons can become inflamed, leading to tendonitis, characterized by pain, stiffness, and weakness when gripping. The pulleys, especially the A1 pulley, can also become dysfunctional, resulting in conditions like trigger finger, where the tendon gets stuck. Pain may manifest as throbbing or aching, and can be accompanied by stiffness in the joints.\n\nIt’s important to monitor your symptoms, and if you experience swelling, redness, or warmth, seeking medical advice would be wise, as these could indicate more serious issues. Taking care of your hands and possibly taping your fingers when you return to climbing can help prevent further injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 3.9722354412078857, "clueweb22-en0035-21-09983:1": 2.069991111755371, "clueweb22-en0000-69-12681:2": 1.4394985437393188, "clueweb22-en0002-28-11753:2": 1.280529260635376, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:8": 1.2718961238861084, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:0": 1.2096389532089233, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 1.183586597442627, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 1.1829979419708252, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 1.1829979419708252, "clueweb22-en0032-55-01112:5": 1.1002365350723267, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:1": 1.072353720664978, "clueweb22-en0002-28-11753:4": 1.0654898881912231, "clueweb22-en0002-28-11753:0": 1.0580270290374756}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 3.9722354412078857, "clueweb22-en0035-21-09983:1": 2.0699