{"metadata": {"team_id": "cfda", "run_id": "gen-only_npsg13_thru0_d4c5", "run_type": "generation-only"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When you start climbing again after an A2 pulley injury, it's important to ease back into it gradually. Here are some key steps to follow:\n\n1. **Rest Period**: Ensure you've taken at least 6 weeks off to allow for proper healing.\n\n2. **Warm-Up**: Always warm up your fingers before climbing. This is crucial for preparing your muscles and tendons for activity.\n\n3. **Start Slowly**: Begin with easier routes that are less finger-intensive. Avoid crimping initially; use open hand grips or half-crimps to reduce strain on your fingers.\n\n4. **Progress Gradually**: Follow a structured finger training regimen over the first four weeks, starting with dead hangs and slowly increasing intensity.\n\n5. **Listen to Your Body**: Pay attention to any discomfort. If you feel pain, take a break and avoid pushing through it.\n\n6. **Rehabilitation Exercises**: Incorporate exercises like resistance bands or finger grippers on your off days to strengthen your fingers.\n\n7. **Pain Management**: Use ice and over-the-counter medications if needed for pain relief.\n\n8. **Consult a Professional**: If you have any concerns about your recovery, consider consulting a doctor or a physical therapist.\n\nBy following these guidelines, you can help ensure a safe return to climbing while minimizing the risk of re-injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:5": 3.370412588119507, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": 2.5946381092071533, "clueweb22-en0006-32-19661:3": 2.1015779972076416, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 1.8839877843856812, "clueweb22-en0014-52-01833:7": 1.8170695304870605, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 1.667868733406067, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:2": 1.4882965087890625, "clueweb22-en0014-52-01833:6": 1.41983163356781, "clueweb22-en0023-98-00232:5": 0.9429137706756592, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 0.682258725166320