{"metadata": {"team_id": "cfda", "run_id": "gen-only_npsg13_thru0_d4c5", "run_type": "generation-only"}, "turn_id": "4-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prepare for your 10-mile run, start by assessing your current fitness level. Since you're not very confident, it's essential to build a solid foundation. Here are some tailored steps for you:\n\n1. **Base Mileage**: If you're currently running 1-3 miles comfortably, spend the first week or two maintaining that distance to establish your base. If you can run 3 miles, you can start your training plan from there.\n\n2. **Structured Training Plan**: Aim for a beginner-friendly 10-mile training plan, like Hal Higdon’s 15K Beginners Training Plan, which is easy to follow. This plan typically spans 8-12 weeks and gradually increases your long run distance.\n\n3. **Long Runs**: Incorporate long runs of 8-10 miles to build endurance. Increase your long run by 20-25% each week to avoid injury.\n\n4. **Frequency**: Aim to run at least three times a week, mixing in easy runs and one day of speed work as you progress.\n\n5. **Cross-Training**: Consider adding 1-2 days of cross-training, such as cycling or swimming, to enhance your overall fitness and reduce injury risk.\n\n6. **Rest and Recovery**: Ensure you have adequate rest days in your schedule to allow your body to recover.\n\n7. **Proper Gear**: Invest in good running shoes and avoid cotton clothing to prevent chafing.\n\n8. **Nutrition and Hydration**: Stay hydrated and maintain a balanced diet to support your training.\n\nBy following these steps and listening to your body, you’ll increase your confidence and readiness for the 10-mile run. Good luck with your training!", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:5": 4.743563175201416, "clueweb22-en0019-87-18451:9": 4.657007694244385, "clueweb22-en0015-88-08032:6": 4.404325485229492, "clueweb22-en0033-80-17520:2": 4.254922389984131, "clueweb22-en0008-57-08667:0": 3.9336886405944824, "clueweb22-en0031-62-14539:1"