{"metadata": {"team_id": "cfda", "run_id": "gen-only_npsg13_thru0_d4c5", "run_type": "generation-only"}, "turn_id": "5-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For a healthier breakfast that aligns with your low-carb preferences, consider incorporating the following habits and ingredients:\n\n1. **High-Protein Options**: Focus on meals rich in protein, such as scrambled eggs, omelettes, or frittata muffins made with vegetables and meats like ham or sausage. These will help keep you full and provide essential nutrients.\n\n2. **Low-Carb Smoothies**: Blend low-carb protein powder with almond milk or coconut milk, and add spinach or berries for flavor and nutrients. This is a quick way to boost your calcium intake as well.\n\n3. **Dairy Sources**: Include foods like Greek yogurt or cottage cheese, which are high in protein and calcium. You can top them with a few berries or a drizzle of natural peanut butter for added flavor.\n\n4. **Breakfast Platter**: Create a cold breakfast plate with hard-boiled eggs, avocado, and cheese. This not only provides healthy fats and protein but also makes for a satisfying meal.\n\n5. **Meal Prep**: Prepare items like Keto Spinach and Bacon Egg Muffins or Chia Pudding in advance. They are nutritious, low in carbs, and can be stored for quick breakfasts throughout the week.\n\n6. **Avoid High-Carb Foods**: Try to limit or replace jam and white bread with low-carb alternatives, such as almond flour pancakes or savory breakfast cookies made with eggs and vegetables.\n\nBy focusing on these options, you'll be able to enjoy delicious breakfasts that are low in carbs and high in protein, while also addressing your calcium needs.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-93-01400:6": 7.3750104904174805, "clueweb22-en0040-12-07702:7": 7.231131553649902, "clueweb22-en0020-68-11239:2": 7.222825050354004, "clueweb22-en0021-99-17494:5": 7.172085762023926, "clueweb22-en0012-69-05596:8": 7.090516090393066, "clueweb22-en0044-06-03044:4": 6.960516452789307, "c